    <div class="article-body">
      <p class="lead">Examine any official Dairy Queen marketing billboard, paper cup, or illuminated menu board in America, and you will notice a conspicuous linguistic absence: the words <strong>"ice cream"</strong> never appear next to the company name. Instead, corporate communications carefully deploy terms like "Soft Serve," "Blizzard® Treats," and "Frozen Dairy Desserts." This is not a marketing whim or casual stylistic branding—it is strict compliance with federal food identity laws enforced by the United States Food and Drug Administration (FDA). Here is the <strong>food biochemistry and regulatory science</strong> behind why Dairy Queen is legally barred from using the ice cream label, and why that exact formula makes it so irresistible.</p>

      <h2>The Legal Mandate: FDA Title 21 CFR § 135.110</h2>
      <p>Under Title 21 of the Code of Federal Regulations, the United States government maintains a strict legal <strong>"Standard of Identity" for Ice Cream</strong>. Originally codified to protect consumers from dairy distributors diluting cream with skim milk and water, the statute sets uncompromising minimum thresholds:</p>
      
      <div style="background:#FAF7F2;border:1px solid #EDE4D6;border-radius:18px;padding:24px;margin:24px 0;">
        <h3 style="margin-top:0;color:#0B1E38;font-size:1.2rem;">The Federal Standard of Identity Rules</h3>
        <ol style="margin:12px 0 0 0;padding-left:20px;line-height:1.7;">
          <li><strong>Mandatory 10% Milkfat Minimum:</strong> Any commercial frozen dessert labeled "ice cream" must contain at least 10% butterfat by weight. Super-premium parlor brands (like Häagen-Dazs or Ben &amp; Jerry’s) typically contain 14% to 18% butterfat.</li>
          <li><strong>Milk Solids Requirement:</strong> The finished product must contain no less than 20% total milk solids.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <p>Dairy Queen’s proprietary soft-serve recipe contains exactly <strong>5% milkfat</strong>. Because its butterfat content is precisely half the federal minimum standard, calling it "ice cream" on any commercial surface would constitute false advertising and misbranding under federal law.</p>

      <h2>Thermodynamic Science: Why 18°F Beats 0°F</h2>
      <p>A casual observer might assume having 5% butterfat instead of 10% makes Dairy Queen inferior. In reality, food biochemists deliberately chose this formulation to solve a major sensory problem inherent to hard ice cream: <strong>sensory numbing</strong>.</p>

