      <h2>The Physics of the Upside-Down Flip</h2>
      <p>How does a Dairy Queen Blizzard defy gravity when the crew member flips it upside-down before your eyes? This is not magic; it is fluid dynamics and colloidal chemistry:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Micro-Air Bubble Entrainment:</strong> The pressurized continuous-feed freezer barrels whip 40% to 45% air into the liquid dairy mix. This process, known as <em>overrun</em>, suspends millions of microscopic air pockets throughout the milkfat emulsion.</li>
        <li><strong>High Yield Stress:</strong> When the Blizzard is blended on the high-speed spindle with chilled mix-ins like crushed cookies or candy, the friction and cold create a <em>thixotropic colloidal suspension</em>. The treat behaves as a solid when resting, resisting gravitational pull until external mechanical stress (your spoon) is applied.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Ingredient Transparency: Debunking Urban Myths</h2>
      <p>Conspiracy theories online frequently claim that Dairy Queen soft serve is made of pig lard, synthetic petroleum oils, or chemical foams. The reality is clean and straightforward:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Real Grade-A Milk &amp; Milkfat:</strong> Delivered in liquid bag-in-box totes from domestic American dairy cooperatives.</li>
        <li><strong>Whey Protein Concentrate:</strong> Natural dairy protein that strengthens the ice crystal framework and prevents syneresis (liquid weeping).</li>
        <li><strong>Natural Plant Hydrocolloids:</strong> Minute quantities of guar gum (from guar beans) and carrageenan (from edible red seaweed) act as natural stabilizers to keep the dairy emulsion homogenous during freezing.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The Mechanics of the Patented DQ "Curl"</h2>
      <p>Every newly inducted Dairy Queen employee spends hours practicing the geometry of the <strong>signature curl</strong>. The process requires establishing a wide circular base on the cone, pulling two concentric ascending spheres, and performing a precise downward compression followed by a sharp, upward wrist-snap. This snap breaks the surface tension of the soft serve at the exact apex, forming the iconic royal curl.</p>

